%I A399120 #23 Oct 21 2026 10:30:42
%S A399120 1,1,3,2,2,8,7,3,6,2,3,5,5,5,4,8,4,4,9,4,3,3,3,4,8,4,6,2,6,6,3,11,7,4,
%T A399120 9,5,9,9,5,5,2,10,7,6,16,8,4,12,8,8,9,4,5,13,9,9,6,5,7,15,7,3,11,3,7,
%U A399120 7,7,8,26,4,12,4,8,8,6,5,6,10,14,6,6,2,10,10,6,11,6,6,6,7
%N A399120 Number of steps starting from the n-th positive integer not divisible by 3 until the first repeated value under the reduced residue-dependent 4k - {1,2} map, or -1 if no value ever repeats.
%C A399120 a(n) is the number of iterations of T, starting from A001651(n), until a value occurs that has already appeared earlier in the same trajectory, where T(k) = (4*k-1)/3^v_3(4*k-1) if k == 1 (mod 3), and T(k) = (4*k-2)/3^v_3(4*k-2) if k == 2 (mod 3).
%C A399120 Here A001651(n) = floor((3*n-1)/2) is the n-th positive integer not divisible by 3, and v_3(m) = A007949(m).
%C A399120 The known positive cycles are the fixed points 1 and 2 and the four-cycle 22 -> 29 -> 38 -> 50 -> 22.
%C A399120 If a trajectory has preperiod length mu and eventual cycle length lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda.
%C A399120 Thus a starting value already at one of the fixed points has value 1, while a starting value already on the four-cycle has value 4.
%C A399120 The linked computation exhaustively verified that every positive starting value k <= 10^11 with 3 not dividing k enters one of the three displayed cycles.
%C A399120 It is not known whether every positive integer not divisible by 3 eventually enters a cycle; additional cycles or divergent trajectories have not been excluded globally.
%H A399120 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="/A399120/b399120.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399120 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22662979">A Ternary (4k - 1(2))/3 Collatz-Type Map: Exact 3-Adic Symbolic Dynamics, a Density-One First-Descent Theorem, Algebraic Cycle Analysis, and Exhaustive Computational Verification up to 10^11</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A399120 Let b(n) = A001651(n) = floor((3*n-1)/2).
%F A399120 a(n) = min { j >= 1 : T^j(b(n)) = T^i(b(n)) for some 0 <= i < j }, if such j exists; otherwise a(n) = -1.
%F A399120 Equivalently, if the trajectory of b(n) has preperiod length mu and eventual period lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda.
%e A399120 For n = 1, A001651(1) = 1 and the orbit begins 1, 1.
%e A399120 The first repeated value occurs after 1 iteration, so a(1) = 1.
%e A399120 For n = 3, A001651(3) = 4 and the orbit is 4, 5, 2, 2.
%e A399120 The first repeated value occurs after 3 iterations, so a(3) = 3.
%e A399120 For n = 6, A001651(6) = 8 and the orbit is 8, 10, 13, 17, 22, 29, 38, 50, 22.
%e A399120 The first repeated value is 22, occurring after 8 iterations, so a(6) = 8.
%e A399120 For n = 15, A001651(15) = 22 and the orbit is 22, 29, 38, 50, 22.
%e A399120 The first repeated value occurs after 4 iterations, so a(15) = 4.
%t A399120 A399120[n_] := Length[NestWhileList[#/3^IntegerExponent[#, 3] & [4*# - Mod[#, 3]] &, Quotient[3*n - 1, 2], UnsameQ, All]] - 1;
%t A399120 Array[A399120, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 21 2026 *)
%o A399120 (Python)
%o A399120 def T(k):
%o A399120     y = 4*k - (k % 3)
%o A399120     while y % 3 == 0:
%o A399120         y //= 3
%o A399120     return y
%o A399120 def a(n):
%o A399120     k, seen = (3*n - 1)//2, set()
%o A399120     while k not in seen:
%o A399120         seen.add(k)
%o A399120         k = T(k)
%o A399120     return len(seen)
%Y A399120 Cf. A001651, A006666, A007949, A400174.
%K A399120 nonn,new
%O A399120 1,3
%A A399120 _Farhad Banazadeh_, Sep 30 2026
